# COMP SUPERSCALAR FRAMEWORK

COMP Superscalar (COMPSs) is a programming model that aims to ease the development of applications for distributed infrastructures, such as Clusters, Grids and Clouds. COMP Superscalar also features a runtime system that exploits the inherent parallelism of applications at execution time.

## Release number: 3.4 (TBD)

Release date: Feb-2026

New features:
    - Upgraded support for Java 21.
    - Upgraded support for Python 3.12.
    - Support for R language (RCOMPSs).
    - New Provenance Run (WRROC) profile recording that generates both prospective and retrospective workflow provenance, with detailed information for each task, even when the application fails (helps debugging).
    - New resource usage plots for CPU and memory when provenance is generated.
    - Support for the usage of sockets between the bindings or agents and the COMPSs runtime.
    - Enabled real-time observability building on OpenTelemetry.
    - New scheduler "es.bsc.compss.scheduler.predefined.PredefinedTS" to enable the explicit scheduling policy.

Improvements:
    - PyCOMPSs CLI inspect largely enhanced to explore provenance metadata: colored interface, resource usage, method profiling, task details, failing tasks and more. Compatible with metadata from Nextflow, Galaxy, CWL, Streamflow, WfExS, Sapporo, Autosubmit, etc.
    - The integration with EAR has been improved for energy consumption metrics gathering and their inclusion into the traces.
    - The integration with DLB has been completed using DROM (Dynamic Resource Ownership Manager) and tested in MareNostrum 5.
    - Improved support for macOS ARM architectures
    - Sandbox management.
    - Configuration files for MN5 HPC systems.
    - Enhanced documentation, with new structure, sections and updated examples.
    - Several Bug fixes.

Deprecations:
    - Dropped support for "generated_jit" decorator from Numba.
    - Support for just-in-time functions within classes has been dropped.

Known Limitations:
    - Dynamic constrains are limited to task parameters declared as IN which are not future objects (generated by previous tasks).
    - Collections are not supported in http tasks.
    - macOS support is limited to Java and Python without CPU affinity (requires executing with --cpu_affinity=disabled). Tracing is not available.
    - Reduce operations can consume more disk space than the manually programmed n-ary reduction.
    - Objects used as task parameters must be serializable.
    - Tasks that invoke Numpy and MKL may experience issues if a different MKL threads count is used in different tasks. This is due to the fact that MKL reuses threads in the different calls and it does not change the number of threads from one call to another. This can be also happen with other libraries implemented with OpenMP. To fix these issues use the DLB option for in the cpu_affinity flag.
    - C++ Objects declared as arguments in coarse-grain tasks must be passed as object pointers in order to have proper dependency management.
    - Master as worker feature is not working for executions with persistent worker in C++.
    - Coherence and concurrent writing in parameters annotated with the "Concurrent" direction must be managed by the underlying distributed storage system.
    - Delete file calls for files used as input can produce a significant synchronization of the main code.
    - Defining a parameter as OUT is only allowed for files and collections of objects with a default constructor.

